Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 14:40:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 096/106] Bluetooth: hci_qca: do not ask for SCO flow control
 by default

The WCN3990 lies about it. It advertises Write Synchronous Flow Control
Enable, accepts the command and afterwards reports the feature as
enabled - but it never sends the completed-packet events that hand the
credits back.

Measured with the quirk on: transmission stops dead after exactly
sco_pkts packets, four of them, while reception carries on at a hundred
a second and the headset goes silent. Left as a module parameter because
the next controller may be honest.
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
index 77f11f261..63e8f8dc5 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -235,10 +235,16 @@ struct qca_serdev {
 };
 
 /*
- * Whether the core may use SCO flow control on this controller. Off turns the
- * quirk back off without a rebuild, to compare the two.
+ * Whether the core may use SCO flow control on this controller.
+ *
+ * Off by default because a WCN3990 lies about it: it advertises Write
+ * Synchronous Flow Control Enable, accepts the command and then reports the
+ * feature as enabled, but never sends the completed-packet events that give
+ * the credits back. Measured: SCO transmission stops dead after exactly
+ * sco_pkts packets - four - while reception carries on at a hundred a second.
+ * Left as a parameter because the next controller may be honest.
  */
-static bool sco_flowctl = true;
+static bool sco_flowctl;
 module_param(sco_flowctl, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(sco_flowctl, "use SCO flow control when the controller offers it");
 
@@ -1953,6 +1959,9 @@ static int qca_setup(struct hci_uart *hu)
 	 *
 	 * The core still checks that the controller advertises Write Sync Flow
 	 * Control before using it, so this is a no-op where it is unsupported.
+	 *
+	 * ⚠ Off by default: this controller advertises the feature and then
+	 * never returns the credits. See the comment on sco_flowctl.
 	 */
 	if (sco_flowctl)
 		hci_set_quirk(hdev, HCI_QUIRK_SYNC_FLOWCTL_SUPPORTED);
